and therefore\n#     several entries, all naming the same descriptor), and each entry names the\n#     array's own descriptor, which says what it holds;\n#   - the mailbox, the outgoing queue, the scheduler and the liveness deadlines are\n#     the L3 Thread's own mechanism, not cells of the record;\n#   - the family chain (parent, first and last child, siblings) is the parent's\n#     supervision data in the language graph;\n#   - the executor's bind record is the executor's.\n# A field that is not one of the four must have an LMX owner in the graph, or go.\n\n# The control flags' type is the one the specification names for them,\n# uint_fast8_t.  On this target that is one byte, which is why the record is 24\n# bytes: 1+1+1, padding to the pointer, 8, 4, padded again.\ntype: LmxFlag uint_fast8_t\n\nstruct: LmxMsg\n    # Permission to continue, and the stop request.  1 while the work is assigned;\n    # cleared at the turn boundary that ends the incarnation.  A 0 read inside a\n    # turn is a stop request, never the Message's own completion (model 31).  One\n    # designated cross-lane writer: the parent stopping a child, or the lane that\n    # takes the turn.\n    LmxFlag: running\n    # Set by the user's code: `success = 1` is a sufficient condition for stopping the\n    # Thread at its end turn.  The system never resets it to 0, not even when a body\n    # fails.  The system sets it to 1 in one place only: for a plain letter, at the\n    # receiver owner's take after the attach (lmx_post_take_inbox).  A heartbeat, an\n", f) < 0
+        return: 1
+    if: c.fputs("    # empty inbox, the absence of a physical thread or the end of a single turn does not\n    # set it (model 31).  A new record starts at 0 (its constructor's initial state).\n    LmxFlag: success\n    # The handoff-safe mark: this Message's storage may be transferred without a\n    # copy.  Read together with the absence of native users (model 32).\n    #\n    # A flag, not an int, and read with the same discipline as `running`: the\n    # decision \"not running and handoff-safe\" reads the two in ONE predicate, and\n    # it is read from the settling side, so a plain field read would let the\n    # compiler keep the load in a register across the loop that waits for it.  The\n    # house spelling for that is a load helper over this type -- the way lmx.h's\n    # poll hook already reads `running` with __atomic_load_n(p, __ATOMIC_RELAXED) --\n    # never a bare `volatile` qualifier: spec 19.29.7.1 sanctions a relaxed atomic\n    # load ONLY where it compiles to the same instructions as an ordinary volatile\n    # load/store, and forbids paying a fence, a read-modify-write or a helper call\n    # beyond that.\n    LmxFlag: handoff_ready\n    # The Message's own graph root: the C projection of the language root.\n    @: Lmx graph\nend: LmxMsg\n", f) < 0
