+    if: c.fputs("# THE WIN32 NAMES THE KERNEL CALLS, declared here instead of <windows.h> (next_core_tasks.md §0\n# «Память сборки», third measurement): the whole header costs cc1 244 MB of parsing in every unit\n# the kernel reaches through predef, for these eleven names.  Exactly the calls and constants the\n# four _win32 bodies use (lmx_clock, lmx_manager_running, lmx_manager_running_lane,\n# lmx_process_deadline), by their Win32 names, with mingw-w64's own types -- DWORD is unsigned\n# long (ulong), BOOL int, HANDLE and LPVOID void *, SIZE_T size_t, ULONGLONG unsigned long long\n# (uint64_t) -- and nothing else: no wrapper, no fallback.  On x64 WINAPI is the one calling\n# convention.  A unit must not also include <windows.h>: two declarations of one function would\n# meet (none of the kernel does).\n\n# size_t and uint64_t, the only standard types below: <windows.h> used to bring them.\ninclude: \"<stddef.h>\" \"<stdint.h>\"\n\ndefine: INFINITE 0xFFFFFFFFU\ndefine: WAIT_OBJECT_0 0U\ndefine: WAIT_TIMEOUT 258U\n\n# LPTHREAD_START_ROUTINE: a thread's body takes its argument and returns a DWORD.\nfnptr: LmxWin32ThreadStart (@: void arg) ulong\n\nprototype:\n    fn: GetTickCount64 () uint64_t\n    sub: Sleep (ulong: ms)\n    fn: WaitForSingleObject (@: void handle; ulong: ms) ulong\n    fn: CloseHandle (@: void handle) int\n    fn: CreateThread (@: void security; size_t: stack; LmxWin32ThreadStart: start; @: void arg; ulong: flags; @: ulong id) @: void\n    fn: GetCurrentThreadId () ulong\n    fn: CreateEventW (@: void security; int: manual; int: initial; @: void name) @: void\n    fn: SetEvent (@: void handle) int\nend: prototype\n", f) < 0
